<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<p><strong>Key teaching:</strong> Virtues are not a &laquo;to-do list.&raquo; They are a way of being. Catechesis on virtues should be positive: it&rsquo;s not about prohibition, but about order. Virtue makes us free to love better.</p>

<p><strong>St. Thomas on prudence:</strong> For Aquinas, prudence is the &laquo;mother of the virtues&raquo; because without it, no other virtue can be exercised well. The prudent person knows when, how, and why to act. It is the virtue of discernment.</p>

<p><strong>Charity as the &laquo;form of the virtues&raquo;:</strong> St. Thomas teaches that charity gives form to all other virtues. Without charity, prudence can become cunning, justice can become rigidity, fortitude can become stubbornness, temperance can become coldness. Charity orders everything to the love of God.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>&#x1F331; Seed (Ages 7-12)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Use the sports metaphor: virtues are like training to be a &laquo;champion of good.&raquo;</li>
  <li>Teach the 4 cardinal virtues as the 4 pillars of a house.</li>
  <li>Activity: draw a house with 4 pillars, write a virtue on each.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F33F; Sprout (Ages 13-17)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Connect to their reality: &laquo;What virtue do you need to resist peer pressure? Fortitude? Prudence?&raquo;</li>
  <li>Discuss how virtues are practiced in small daily decisions.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1FAB4; Root (Adults new in faith)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>May come from a context where morality was just &laquo;rules.&raquo;</li>
  <li>Help them see virtues as a path to freedom, not restriction.</li>
</ul>

<h4>&#x1F333; Tree (Formed adults)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Develop the relationship between theological virtues and the gifts of the Holy Spirit.</li>
  <li>Discuss whether non-Christians can live the cardinal virtues. What do they lack without charity?</li>
</ul>

</section>

