<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<p><strong>1. &laquo;Aren&rsquo;t virtues just for saints? I can&rsquo;t be perfect.&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>Virtues are not instant perfection. They are a path. No one is born virtuous. What matters is not being perfect, but being on the way. A small act of virtue each day brings you closer to God.</p>

<p><strong>2. &laquo;What if I don&rsquo;t have faith? Can I be virtuous without God?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>Yes, you can have cardinal virtues without explicit faith. Many non-Christians live these virtues well. But without charity (a gift from God), these virtues don&rsquo;t lead to union with God. They are like a car without fuel: it works, but doesn&rsquo;t reach its final destination.</p>

<p><strong>3. &laquo;Sometimes I do good and feel nothing. Does that mean I don&rsquo;t have virtue?&raquo;</strong></p>
<p>No. Virtue is not a feeling; it is a habit. You can do good without feeling anything and still grow in virtue. In fact, virtuous acts done when you don&rsquo;t &laquo;feel&raquo; them are the ones that most strengthen the virtue.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist&rsquo;s Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>
Lord God, source of all virtue,<br />
You who have called us to be holy,<br />
grant us the grace to grow in the virtues.<br />
May we be prudent in our decisions,<br />
just in our actions,<br />
strong in difficulties,<br />
and temperate in the use of Your gifts.<br />
Increase in us faith, hope, and charity,<br />
and make us bear fruit for eternal life.<br />
Amen.
</p>
</blockquote>

